From 80050f24f9a83d6f3388500a77485c9f8af1472f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mark Harfouche <mark.harfouche@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 21:37:11 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Export the C++ API of the shared libraries explicitly

On Windows, the SHARED build of SPIRV-Tools and the libraries layered on
top of it (opt, link, lint, reduce, diff) did not export their C++ API,
so it could only be used with CMAKE_WINDOWS_EXPORT_ALL_SYMBOLS, which
exports everything but never dllimports data, vtables or static members.

Give each library its own export macro in libspirv.h, driven by
SPIRV_TOOLS_<LIB>_SHAREDLIB (public) and SPIRV_TOOLS_<LIB>_IMPLEMENTATION
(private) compile definitions that spvtools_export_macros() sets on shared
library targets, and annotate:
- the public C++ API (optimizer.hpp, linker.hpp, linter.hpp) and the
  optimizer C API, which is defined in SPIRV-Tools-opt;
- the internals of SPIRV-Tools used by the other libraries and tools;
- the internals of SPIRV-Tools-opt used by the other libraries, tools and
  by consumers of the spvtools::opt API such as Tint (IRContext, Module,
  Function, BasicBlock, Instruction, the analyses, types, constants and
  a few passes).

MSVC defines the implicit copy operations of dllexport'ed classes and
instantiates every member of their template base classes, so:
- ConstantManager, PassManager and InstructionDisassembler explicitly
  delete their copy operations, which cannot compile for their members;
- the move operations of IntrusiveNodeBase cast `this` to NodeType*, as
  they would not compile when instantiated.
